[?2004l[?2004h> # Performance Fix Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Problem
[?2004l[?2004h> JSON validator was processing at ~40 docs/sec instead of the expected 400-500 docs/sec.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Root Cause
[?2004l[?2004h> The `validate_document()` function was calling `preprocess_schema()` and creating a new `Draft7Validator` for EVERY document validation. With 200 test documents:
[?2004l[?2004h> - 200 jq subprocess calls (expensive)
[?2004l[?2004h> - 200 validator compilations (expensive)
[?2004l[?2004h> - No reuse of preprocessed schemas or compiled validators
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Solution
[?2004l[?2004h> Implemented validator caching via `get_validator()` function:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Added `_validator_cache` dictionary to store compiled validators by schema path
[?2004l[?2004h> - Each unique schema is now only preprocessed once via jq
[?2004l[?2004h> - Each validator is only compiled once and reused for all documents using that schema
[?2004l[?2004h> - With 50 schemas and 200 documents, this reduced operations from 200 to ~50
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Results
[?2004l[?2004h> - Before: 40.2 docs/sec
[?2004l[?2004h> - After: ~1420 docs/sec
[?2004l[?2004h> - Improvement: 35x faster
[?2004l[?2004h> - Target met: Yes (required 400+ docs/sec)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Code Changes
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Added `_validator_cache = {}` global cache
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Created `get_validator(schema_path)` function to manage cached validators
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Modified `validate_document()` to use `get_validator()` instead of calling `preprocess_schema()` directly
